A||||9||Jncomutt||High-Flow Cat.||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||10-31-2001||10:59 AM||||OK, After all the posts and stories and expieriences shared here on the forum, I've decided I'm just going to keep my cat on the car.  What I'm looking to find out is where I can find the free-est (if thats a word) flowing cat out there.  Its been posted that the newer hi-flo cats have very little restriction compared to my stock 84.  <P>So, can I just go down to the local auto store and pick one up?  Or should I get one from Rodney, or someplace else?  And are there differences between them?  Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||10-31-2001||11:18 AM||||I just went to the (non)Local Car-X and had them replace it when they did the rest of my custom exhaust <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013191.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013191.html</A> <P>...just told them I wanted a High-Flow cat, and they said OK!<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||10-31-2001||11:48 AM||||I got mine from the Fiero Store. When you look inside it all you see is a thin wire mesh. I guess this makes it  a flow thru design with very little restriction.||||reg||1
Z||000003||VISCERAL||10-31-2001||05:10 PM||||Anything other than factory brick style is a hiflow cat. Try catco for usually $50 from <A HREF="http://www.summit-racing.com." TARGET=_blank>www.summit-racing.com.</A>  or ebay sometimes. Get the size you need to match the original.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Turbo88||10-31-2001||05:23 PM||||I've heard bad things about the catco's from a friend of mine who owns an exhaust shop. I had one on my Formula and it had a chunk break off inside. Quite an interesing sound actually. Anyway, we were talking about them and he said he used to sell them to the aftermarket crowd but had a lot of them come back. Before he told me this, I had always thought of catco as a good brand to buy. Hmmm... Thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000005||avengador1||10-31-2001||09:39 PM||||Rodney Dickman has high flow 409 stainless steel 2-1/4" in/out catalytic converters with extensions to replace the original stock units. His website is <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||WhiteFieroBlur||11-01-2001||01:42 AM||||I was just going to go with the high flow cat with the spin tech muffler from west coast. I really wanna go with the straight pipe but I have no one to install it for me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jncomutt||11-01-2001||09:01 AM||||From the pics on the site, it looks like Rodney uses catco.  I don't think he would sell a product that would fail however.  Any expieriences with his cat anyone??||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||11-01-2001||09:05 AM||||West Coast Fiero also has a hi flo cat, and it's available in different pipe sizes, if you are building a custom exhaust.||||reg||1
Z||000009||cdunn||11-01-2001||10:15 AM||||I put the rodney cat on my 88 duke about 10 months ago and i've had no troubles with it. I just sawzalled the stock unit and put the rodney one in with clamps. I also saw a straight pipe on his web site that had clamp connections.||||reg||1
